<section xml:id="section-compactness-and-connectedness" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Compactness and Connectedness [SKIP]
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Recall that a subset <m> U \subseteq X </m> of a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m> is (sequentially) compact if every sequence of points in <m> U </m> has a subsequence which converges to a limit in <m> U </m>. In this section, we investigate two analogues of this property for general topological spaces.
    </p>
  </introduction>

